<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Percy Bysshe Shelley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Percy Bysshe Shelley was an English writer considered one of the major English Romantic poets, born in 1792 at Field Place, Warnham, Sussex, the eldest son of a Whig Member of Parliament. A radical in his poetry and in his political and social views, he faced a backlash against his avowed atheism and went into permanent self-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1818. His works include Ozymandias (1818), The Mask of Anarchy (1819), Ode to the West Wind (1819), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prometheus">Prometheus</a> Unbound (1820) and Adonais (1821). His second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a>, was the author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a>. He died in a boating accident in 1822, aged 29, and his poems later found admirers in Owenist, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartist</a> and radical circles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley.jpg" alt="Percy Bysshe Shelley" width="330" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Amelia Curran · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Percy_Bysshe_Shelley_by_Alfred_Clint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some found their way back to faith, like François-René de Chateaubriand, whose The Genius of Christianity of 1802 defended the Catholic religion through its beauty, or Friedrich Schlegel, who became a Catholic in 1808; others, like <b>Percy Bysshe Shelley</b>, were rebels against Church and state.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the trumpets which sing to battle and feel not what they inspire; the influence which is moved not, but moves. Poets are the unacknowledged legislators of the world.&#8221;</p><footer>A Defence of Poetry (written 1821, published 1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Prose_Works_of_Percy_Bysshe_Shelley/Volume_2/A_Defence_of_Poetry" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Poetry is the record of the best and happiest moments of the happiest and best minds.&#8221;</p><footer>A Defence of Poetry (written 1821, published 1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Prose_Works_of_Percy_Bysshe_Shelley/Volume_2/A_Defence_of_Poetry" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
